  "voiceover": "Is Jesus God in the New Testament? Don't pull on one verse — pull on all of them at once. The New Testament isn't a single line; it's a library of twenty-seven documents, written by independent authors, across half a century, in two languages. In a locked room, Thomas reaches toward the wounds of the risen Jesus and says what no devout Jew said to anyone but the LORD: My Lord and my God. John opens his Gospel reaching behind Genesis: in the beginning was the Word, and the Word was God. Paul says that in Christ the whole fullness of deity dwells bodily. Hebrews records the Father speaking to the Son: Your throne, O God, is forever and ever. And Revelation gives Jesus the LORD's own name from Isaiah: I am the first and the last. Then look at what they did — they worshipped him, the honor angels themselves refuse. And it was early. Marana tha — our Lord, come — is Aramaic, pre-Pauline, from the Jerusalem church of the thirties. Not a late legend. Independent authors, two languages, half a century, all placing Jesus inside the identity of the one God of Israel. The creed's vocabulary came later. The evidence it organized was already here. God in the flesh — written from the start.",
